WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.270
Hi, I'm Fid!

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:05.862
Here's how a Mac laptop running Linux drives an Intel card

00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:07.169
through one Thunderbolt cable.

00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:11.891
The model never crosses the cable.

00:00:11.891 --> 00:00:16.264
What slowed it was the driver's own small buffers, left on the laptop's side.

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:22.229
The laptop runs Linux with sixteen kilobyte pages.

00:00:22.229 --> 00:00:25.366
Its port carries PCI Express inside a tunnel.

00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:29.252
The tunnel ends in a powered box, with the card in a normal slot.

00:00:29.672 --> 00:00:33.193
On top, llama dot c p p talks to Intel's Vulkan driver,

00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:34.953
which sends the card its work.

00:00:35.373 --> 00:00:38.217
Every layer of the model sits in the card's own memory.

00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:44.372
Intel's kernel driver assumes host pages and GPU pages are the same size.

00:00:44.792 --> 00:00:49.607
The patches keep GPU units at four kilobytes, and round host buffers to sixteen.

00:00:51.292 --> 00:00:53.888
The weights and cache were in card memory from the start.

00:00:54.344 --> 00:00:58.214
But the CPU writes shader code, descriptors and state, so they landed

00:00:58.214 --> 00:00:59.504
on the laptop's side.

00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:02.520
Every small dispatch fetched them across the cable.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.090
Moving them took a small model from about nineteen to sixty

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.389
six tokens a second.

00:01:08.842 --> 00:01:12.161
That is ninety four percent of the same card in a desktop slot.

00:01:14.525 --> 00:01:15.649
Here is the formula.

00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:20.400
Decode reads every weight once per token, so divide the card's bandwidth

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.434
by the weights on the card.

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:24.586
That ceiling is about thirty tokens a second.

00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:29.686
The measured seventeen point two eight is fifty seven percent of it.

00:01:29.686 --> 00:01:32.682
A native card on this backend lands about the same.

00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:35.971
Over a tunnel, bulk bandwidth is fine.

00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:39.238
Small reads a driver assumes are local are what cost you.

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:45.503
Weights on the card, driver buffers moved, and the patches still private.

00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:48.355
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.355 --> 00:01:48.933
I'm Fid.

00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:49.222
Bye!
